{
  "passage": "In most villages of India, local matters are managed by an elected body called the Gram Panchayat. Every adult who lives in the village and whose name is on the voters' list is a member of the Gram Sabha, the meeting of all such people. The Gram Sabha elects the members of the Gram Panchayat, who are called ward members or panches, and the head of the panchayat, called the sarpanch or pradhan. They are elected for five years. The Gram Panchayat also has a secretary, who is appointed by the government and is not elected. The secretary calls the meetings of the Gram Sabha and keeps a record of what is discussed. The Gram Panchayat looks after the construction and repair of village roads, drains, schools and water sources, and it collects some local taxes. It also carries out government schemes for employment and housing. The Gram Sabha plays an important role in keeping the panchayat honest. At its meetings, villagers can question the sarpanch about how money was spent and can suggest new work. One third of the seats in panchayats are reserved for women, and many states have raised this to half. Above the village level, a group of Gram Panchayats forms the Panchayat Samiti at the block level, and the Zila Parishad works at the district level.",
